(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-physiology-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form A","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the fundamental principle Claude Bernard articulated with the concept of the \"milieu interieur\"?","choices":["Disease is simply the study of disordered body function at the cellular level","Cells reproduce and divide continuously via the process of mitosis","Genes alone control all cellular behavior, independent of the extracellular environment","The function of the whole body is much more than the sum of its parts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Homeostasis is the maintenance of a stable \"milieu interieur,\" and Bernard's fundamental principle is that the function of the whole body is much more than the sum of its parts. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is pathophysiology?","choices":["The study of disordered body function (disease), the basis for clinical medicine","The study of feedback control mechanisms only, within otherwise healthy tissue","The study of genetics and inherited chromosomal disorders exclusively","The study of normal cell organelles and their function in healthy tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pathophysiology is the study of disordered body function (i.e., disease), and it forms the basis for clinical medicine. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Approximately how many cells make up the human body?","choices":["~35 to 40 trillion","~10 million","~100 trillion","~35 to 40 billion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cells are the basic structural and functional unit of the body, numbering approximately 35 to 40 trillion. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"The baroreceptor reflex response to hemorrhage (increased sympathetic activity, increased heart rate, vasoconstriction to restore arterial pressure) is an example of what kind of control?","choices":["Negative feedback, promoting stability","Feed-forward control, anticipating pressure change","Positive feedback, amplifying the pressure change","No feedback control involved whatsoever"],"answer":0,"exp":"The baroreceptor reflex's response to hemorrhage/decreased arterial pressure is negative feedback, which promotes stability by counteracting the change. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Cardiopulmonary reflexes that anticipate an upcoming change in blood pressure (rather than responding after the fact) are examples of what kind of control?","choices":["Positive feedback","Feed-forward control","Negative feedback","Genomic control"],"answer":1,"exp":"Feed-forward control anticipates change, as with cardiopulmonary reflexes, in contrast to feedback control which responds after a change has occurred. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Hemorrhagic shock and the depolarization phase of the action potential are both classic physiological examples of what type of feedback?","choices":["No feedback","Negative feedback","Positive feedback","Feed-forward control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Both hemorrhagic shock (a dangerous, self-amplifying cycle) and the action potential's rapid depolarization are examples of positive feedback, where the response amplifies the initial change. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which of the following is most precisely, tightly controlled in the body?","choices":["Plasma [H+] concentration","Systemic blood flow","Arterial blood pressure","Basal metabolic rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Some variables, like plasma [H+] concentration (~3-5 x 10^-8 moles/L), are precisely controlled, while others like blood pressure, blood flow, and metabolic rate vary more with sex, age, body size, and circadian rhythms. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What percentage of cell mass is typically composed of water?","choices":["70-85%","10-20%","2-95%","1-6%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Water makes up 70-85% of cell mass, with proteins at 10-20%, lipids at 2-95% (highly variable by cell type), and carbohydrates at 1-6%. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What distinguishes rough (granular) ER from smooth ER?","choices":["Rough ER is found only within the nucleus, forming a direct continuation of the nuclear envelope","They are functionally identical structures, both synthesising lipids and processing newly made proteins equally","Rough ER has ribosomes on its outer surface and processes newly synthesized proteins; smooth ER is the site of lipid synthesis","Rough ER is the site of lipid synthesis; smooth ER has ribosomes covering its outer surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rough (granular) ER has ribosomes covering its outer surface and extrudes/processes newly synthesized proteins into its matrix, while smooth ER is the site of lipid synthesis (phospholipids, cholesterol). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What happens to substances after they arrive at the Golgi apparatus from the smooth ER?","choices":["They are used directly to form the new strands of nuclear DNA during cell replication","They are processed (phosphorylated, glycosylated), then concentrated, sorted, and packaged for secretion","They are immediately degraded entirely by lysosomal acid hydrolase enzymes","They are immediately released directly into the nucleus for permanent storage"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Golgi apparatus receives transport vesicles from the smooth ER and processes substances (phosphorylation, glycosylation) before concentrating, sorting, and packaging them for secretion. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the difference between constitutive and stimulated secretion?","choices":["Constitutive secretion requires a trigger; stimulated secretion happens randomly","Constitutive secretion happens randomly; stimulated secretion requires a trigger","They are the same process","Only stimulated secretion involves the Golgi apparatus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Constitutive secretion happens randomly (continuously), while stimulated secretion requires a specific trigger before secretory vesicles release their contents. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What do lysosomes contain, and what is their primary function?","choices":["DNA polymerase and helicase enzymes, used for unwinding and then replicating the nuclear DNA","Hydrolytic enzymes (acid hydrolases) such as phosphatases, nucleases, proteases, and lysozymes, for digesting material","Oxidase enzymes exclusively, for detoxifying alcohol and other toxic compounds","Free ribosomes and rRNA, for synthesizing new cytoplasmic proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lysosomes are vesicular organelles formed from budding Golgi that contain hydrolytic acid hydrolases (phosphatases, nucleases, proteases, lipid-degrading enzymes, lysozymes) and fuse with endocytotic vesicles to digest their contents. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why is the resting membrane potential of a typical neuron so close to the potassium equilibrium potential (EK)?","choices":["The membrane is far more permeable to Na+ than K+ at rest, due to roughly 100 times more Na+ leak channels than K+ leak channels being present","There are no leak channels at rest; the resting potential instead arises purely from the continuous activity of the Na+/K+ ATPase pump alone","Na+ and K+ permeability are equal at rest, causing the resting membrane potential to sit almost exactly midway between ENa and EK","The membrane is far more permeable to K+ than Na+ (about 100x more K+ leak channels)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The resting membrane potential is close to EK because the membrane is far more permeable to K+ than Na+ at rest, due to roughly 100x more K+ leak channels than Na+ leak channels. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Why is the resting membrane potential of red blood cells (RBCs) so close to 0 mV, unlike most other cells?","choices":["RBCs have unusually few Cl-/HCO3- exchanger proteins compared to most other cell membranes","Resting membrane permeability to sodium ions is high compared to most other cell types","RBCs actively pump chloride out via an ATP-dependent transporter absent from most other cell types","RBCs have far fewer K+ leak channels relative to Na+ leak channels than most other cell types"],"answer":1,"exp":"The resting membrane potential of RBCs is close to 0 mV because their resting membrane permeability to sodium ions is high compared to most other cell types (unlike neurons, which are much more K+-permeable at rest). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How is the electrochemical driving force (VDF) on an ion calculated, and what does a positive VDF indicate?","choices":["VDF = Eion - Vm; a positive VDF always means no net movement","VDF = Vm - Eion; a positive VDF means the ion enters the cell (influx)","VDF = Vm - Eion; a positive VDF means the ion exits the cell (efflux)","VDF cannot be calculated for individual ions"],"answer":2,"exp":"The electrochemical driving force (VDF) for any ion equals Vm - Eion; a positive VDF means the ion exits the cell (efflux), while a negative VDF means the ion enters the cell (influx). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How does an increase in extracellular potassium concentration affect cell excitability, and why?","choices":["It decreases excitability by hyperpolarizing the cell, since a higher extracellular K+ concentration pulls the resting potential further from threshold","It increases excitability because the resting membrane potential moves closer to the threshold potential","It has no measurable effect on excitability, since threshold potential shifts in the exact opposite direction to compensate for any change in resting potential","It only affects skeletal muscle excitability, since neurons regulate their resting potential independently of extracellular potassium concentration"],"answer":1,"exp":"An increase in extracellular potassium concentration depolarizes the resting membrane potential, bringing it closer to the threshold potential and thus increasing cell excitability. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the effect of low plasma calcium on threshold potential and excitability?","choices":["Low calcium abolishes the action potential entirely by permanently inactivating voltage-gated sodium channels throughout the axon hillock","Threshold potential is completely unaffected by calcium concentration, changing instead only in response to shifts in extracellular potassium concentration","Threshold potential becomes more positive, moving further from resting Vm and decreasing excitability, which is why hypercalcemia can cause muscle weakness","Threshold potential becomes more negative (closer to resting Vm), increasing excitability"],"answer":3,"exp":"Low plasma calcium makes the threshold potential more negative, bringing it closer to the resting membrane potential and thus increasing cell excitability (this is why hypocalcemia can cause tetany/spontaneous firing). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which of the following is a defining property of the action potential?","choices":["It decays exponentially with distance from its point of origin, much like a graded electrotonic potential spreading passively along the membrane","It is an all-or-none event with constant amplitude that does not summate","It requires no voltage-gated channels at all, propagating instead purely through passive electrotonic spread of current along the axon membrane","It is a graded event whose amplitude varies continuously with stimulus strength, much like an excitatory postsynaptic potential at the synapse"],"answer":1,"exp":"The action potential is an all-or-none event (must reach threshold) with constant amplitude — it does not summate, and it propagates without decrement by relying on voltage-gated ion channels. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"According to the rule of thumb given, how is the conduction velocity of a myelinated fiber estimated from its diameter?","choices":["Velocity (m/s) = fiber diameter (mm) x 4.5","Velocity (m/s) = square root of fiber diameter for myelinated fibers","Velocity is unrelated to fiber diameter","Velocity (m/s) = diameter (mm) squared"],"answer":0,"exp":"For myelinated fibers, conduction velocity (m/s) is approximately fiber diameter (mm) x 4.5; for unmyelinated fibers, velocity is approximated by the square root of the fiber diameter. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why do local anesthetics typically produce analgesia (pain relief) without paralysis?","choices":["They preferentially block only large motor neurons, since motor axons have a much lower threshold for local anesthetic binding than the small unmyelinated sensory fibers carrying pain","They only work on heavily myelinated fibers, since local anesthetics require access to the node of Ranvier and cannot cross the continuous membrane of unmyelinated C-fibers","They are more effective against small-diameter neurons with a large surface-area-to-volume ratio, so small C-fibers (pain) are affected more than large motor neurons","They have no selective effect based on fiber size at all, blocking large motor neurons and small pain fibers at exactly the same drug concentration and rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Local anesthetics are more effective against small-diameter neurons with a large surface-area-to-volume ratio, so small C-fibers carrying pain sensation are blocked more readily than large alpha-motorneurons, producing analgesia without paralysis. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What happens to Na+ and K+ permeability during the upstroke of the action potential?","choices":["Both Na+ and K+ permeability decrease sharply as their voltage-gated channels close in direct response to the initial depolarizing stimulus itself","Only K+ permeability increases during the upstroke, opening K+ channels that drive the membrane potential rapidly and directly toward EK","Neither Na+ nor K+ permeability changes during the upstroke, which is instead generated entirely by passive capacitive current across the membrane","Na+ permeability increases (opening Na+ channels, moving Vm toward ENa); K+ permeability increase follows, moving Vm toward EK"],"answer":3,"exp":"During the upstroke of the action potential, Na+ permeability increases due to opening Na+ channels, moving the membrane potential toward ENa; K+ permeability then increases due to opening K+ channels, moving the potential toward EK. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What are the three main stimuli that cause ion channels to \"gate\" (transition between conducting and non-conducting states)?","choices":["Only ligand binding to a specific receptor protein, since voltage changes and mechanical forces alone cannot independently open any ion channel","Temperature, pH, and osmolarity changes, which together account for essentially all physiological gating of ion channels in excitable tissue","Changes in membrane potential (voltage-gated), occupation of a receptor (ligand-gated), and mechanical forces (mechanosensitive)","Only changes in membrane potential, since ligand binding and mechanical deformation cannot independently trigger channel gating in any tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Ion channels gate in response to changes in membrane potential (voltage-gated channels), occupation of a receptor (ligand-gated/receptor-operated channels), or mechanical forces (mechanosensitive channels, important for hearing). — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How do voltage-gated Na+ channels differ from K+ channels in terms of inactivation during depolarization?","choices":["K+ channels inactivate substantially faster than Na+ channels, closing within about a millisecond of depolarization and terminating the repolarizing current early","Both channel types show voltage inactivation during depolarization, entering a nonconducting state that must be reset by repolarization before reopening","Neither channel type shows activation in response to depolarization; both instead open passively whenever the membrane potential drifts near rest","Depolarization causes Na+ channels to both activate and inactivate; K+ channels show activation but not inactivation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Depolarization causes Na+ channels to activate (open) but also to inactivate (enter a nonconducting state); by contrast, K+ channels show activation but not inactivation — they simply deactivate (close) upon repolarization. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the difference between the absolute and relative refractory periods?","choices":["They are identical concepts, both describing the brief window immediately after an action potential during which Na+ channels remain fully available","Absolute refractory period requires a greater-than-normal stimulus due to partial Na+ channel inactivation; relative refractory period makes any new action potential completely impossible","Absolute refractory period: AP is impossible due to Na+ channel voltage inactivation; relative refractory period: a greater-than-normal stimulus is required","Refractory periods only apply to K+ channels, since Na+ channels reset instantly and can reopen immediately regardless of membrane potential"],"answer":2,"exp":"During the absolute refractory period, an action potential is impossible due to voltage inactivation of Na+ channels; during the relative refractory period, a stimulus greater than normal is required to elicit an action potential. — Lecture 2 - Cell Membrane Potentials and Action Potentials.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which type of sensory receptor detects deformation of tissue?","choices":["Chemoreceptors","Thermoreceptors","Mechanoreceptors","Nociceptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Mechanoreceptors detect mechanical deformation, distinct from thermoreceptors (temperature change), nociceptors (tissue damage/pain), electromagnetic receptors (light), and chemoreceptors (taste, smell, CO2, O2). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the \"labeled line principle\" of sensory perception?","choices":["Only pain receptors follow this principle, while all other receptor types transmit multiple different sensory modalities along a single shared pathway","Sensory information from every receptor type converges onto a single shared thalamic relay before random distribution to the cortex","All receptors respond equally to every type of stimulus, with modality determined only by the frequency of firing rather than which fibers are activated","Each receptor responds to a limited range of stimuli and has a direct line to the brain, giving specificity of nerve fibers for transmitting only one modality of sensation"],"answer":3,"exp":"The labeled-line principle is the concept that each receptor responds to a limited range of stimuli and has a direct line to the brain, giving specificity of nerve fibers for transmitting only one modality of sensation. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why does a Pacinian corpuscle generate a receptor (electrotonic) potential rather than an action potential directly at the receptor?","choices":["The receptor membrane lacks any mechanically-gated ion channels","Pacinian corpuscles depolarize but never repolarize afterward","There are no voltage-gated Na+ channels except in the axon","Receptor potentials are functionally identical to action potentials"],"answer":2,"exp":"Mechanical distortion of a Pacinian corpuscle increases Na+ conductance, generating an electrotonic receptor potential; an action potential only occurs once this signal reaches the axon, where voltage-gated Na+ channels are present. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How does increased stimulus intensity affect action potential frequency in a sensory neuron?","choices":["Increased stimulus intensity causes an increased receptor potential, which increases AP frequency","Increased stimulus intensity increases the amplitude of individual action potentials rather than their frequency","Increased stimulus intensity has no effect on AP frequency, only on the duration of the receptor potential","Increased stimulus intensity decreases AP frequency by triggering rapid receptor desensitization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Action potentials occur once the receptor potential rises above threshold; increased stimulus intensity causes an increased receptor potential, which in turn increases the frequency of action potentials generated. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is receptor adaptation, and how does the Pacinian corpuscle mechanism work?","choices":["The gradual decrease in receptor response to a sustained stimulus; in the Pacinian corpuscle, fluid redistribution decreases the distorting force over time","Adaptation means the receptor becomes progressively more sensitive to a sustained stimulus; in the Pacinian corpuscle, fluid accumulation increases the distorting force over time","Receptors always fire at a constant, unchanging rate regardless of stimulus duration, since fluid within the Pacinian corpuscle cannot redistribute","Adaptation only occurs in photoreceptors and chemoreceptors, never in mechanoreceptors like the Pacinian corpuscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Receptors adapt (respond less) to a sustained stimulus; in the Pacinian corpuscle, fluid redistribution within the corpuscle decreases the distorting force on the nerve ending over time, while photoreceptors adapt via changes in the amount of light-sensitive chemicals. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What are the two mechanisms by which gradations in sensory signal intensity are achieved?","choices":["Only changes in receptor potential amplitude, since spatial and temporal summation do not occur outside the central nervous system","Only changes in axon diameter, which alter conduction velocity but not the perceived intensity of a sensory signal","Spatial summation (increasing the number of fibers stimulated) and temporal summation (increasing the firing rate in a given number of fibers)","Adaptation and facilitation only, without any contribution from either the number or firing rate of stimulated fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gradations in signal intensity are achieved by spatial summation (increasing the number of fibers stimulated) and temporal summation (increasing the rate of firing in a given number of fibers). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What are neuronal pools?","choices":["Groups of neurons with special characteristics of organization, comprising many different types of neuronal circuits (converging, diverging, reverberating, inhibitory)","Groups of neurons found only in the spinal cord gray matter that mediate simple monosynaptic reflexes and nothing more complex than that","A specialized type of glial cell that provides structural and metabolic support to surrounding groups of neurons within the brain","Isolated single neurons with no synaptic connections to any other neuron, functioning entirely independently of neuronal circuits"],"answer":0,"exp":"Neuronal pools are groups of neurons with special characteristics of organization, comprising many different types of neuronal circuits, including converging, diverging, reverberating, and inhibitory circuits. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is an example of the \"amplifying\" type of divergence in neuronal pathways?","choices":["A single sensory neuron branching to synapse on only two or three second-order neurons","Multiple sensory inputs converging onto a single interneuron in the dorsal horn","Reciprocal inhibition of an antagonist muscle pair during a simple stretch reflex","A single pyramidal cell in the motor cortex stimulating several hundred muscle fibers"],"answer":3,"exp":"An amplifying type of divergence is illustrated by a single pyramidal cell in the motor cortex that can stimulate several hundred muscle fibers, spreading a single signal to many targets. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does convergence of multiple input fibers onto a single neuron provide?","choices":["Faster conduction velocity only, with no change to the pattern or timing of incoming signal integration","Spatial summation, allowing correlation, summation, and sorting of information from multiple sources","Reduction of all incoming signals to a single averaged output, discarding information from individual sources","Only inhibitory effects, canceling out excitatory input from every converging presynaptic fiber"],"answer":1,"exp":"Convergence, where multiple terminals from different incoming fibers terminate on the same neuron, provides spatial summation, allowing the neuron to correlate, summate, and sort information arriving from multiple sources. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the function of the reciprocal inhibition circuit?","choices":["To increase synaptic delay between the cord and brainstem reflex centers","To control antagonistic pairs of muscles and prevent over-activity in the brain","To amplify excitatory signals equally to both agonist and antagonist muscles","To generate spontaneous action potentials independent of any presynaptic input"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inhibitory (reciprocal inhibition) circuit is important for controlling all antagonistic pairs of muscles and for preventing over-activity in the brain. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What causes a reverberatory (oscillatory) circuit to produce a prolonged output after a brief single input stimulus, and what eventually stops it?","choices":["Positive feedback within the circuit re-excites the input; fatigue of synaptic junctions causes sudden cessation","Output stops only when the presynaptic terminal is permanently destroyed by excitotoxic calcium overload","Negative feedback within the circuit immediately dampens the signal, ending output within milliseconds of the stimulus","Divergence alone accounts for the prolonged output, requiring no feedback mechanism of any kind"],"answer":0,"exp":"A reverberatory circuit produces prolonged output via positive feedback, where the circuit re-excites its own input; the reverberation eventually stops due to fatigue of synaptic junctions (transmitter depletion, receptor inactivation, abnormal ion concentrations). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How does synaptic underactivity versus overactivity affect membrane receptor number?","choices":["Receptor number is fixed and never changes regardless of the level of presynaptic activity at that synapse","Both underactivity and overactivity cause downregulation, reducing sensitivity in either direction","Underactivity leads to upregulation of membrane receptors; overactivity leads to downregulation","Underactivity leads to rapid downregulation; overactivity leads to sustained upregulation of receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Underactivity of a synapse leads to upregulation of membrane receptors (increasing sensitivity), while overactivity leads to downregulation of membrane receptors (decreasing sensitivity) — a homeostatic mechanism. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the key functional difference between alpha and gamma motor neurons?","choices":["Gamma motor neurons (large type A-gamma fibers) innervate large extrafusal fibers to generate contractile force; alpha motor neurons (small type A-alpha fibers) innervate small intrafusal fibers to set muscle tone","Alpha motor neurons are smaller in diameter than gamma motor neurons, conducting signals more slowly and innervating only the small intrafusal spindle fibers","Alpha motor neurons innervate large extrafusal fibers (skeletal muscle force); gamma motor neurons innervate small intrafusal fibers in the muscle spindle (controlling muscle tone)","Both alpha and gamma motor neurons innervate the exact same fiber types with identical thresholds, differing only in total synapse count per fiber"],"answer":2,"exp":"Alpha motor neurons (large type A-alpha fibers, ~14 microns) innervate large extrafusal fibers that generate force for muscle contraction, while gamma motor neurons (smaller type A-gamma fibers, ~5 microns) innervate small intrafusal fibers within the muscle spindle to control basic muscle tone. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is a motor unit, as defined via the alpha motor neuron?","choices":["An alpha motor neuron plus all the skeletal muscle fibers it innervates (which can range from 3 to over 100 fibers)","A single gamma motor neuron together with the small intrafusal muscle spindle fibers it exclusively innervates and controls","A single denervated extrafusal muscle fiber with no innervation, generating spontaneous fibrillation potentials due to membrane instability","The muscle spindle alone, considered independently of any surrounding extrafusal fibers or their alpha motor neuron innervation"],"answer":0,"exp":"A motor unit is the alpha motor neuron plus the skeletal (extrafusal) muscle fibers it innervates after branching within the muscle — stimulation can excite anywhere from 3 to over 100 fibers depending on the unit. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the function of Renshaw cells, and what negative feedback mechanism do they mediate?","choices":["They are excitatory glutamatergic interneurons in the anterior horn that amplify alpha motor neuron output, forming a positive feedback loop with no inhibitory component at all","They only affect gamma motor neurons, receiving an excitatory collateral exclusively from gamma axons and feeding back solely to the muscle spindle system","They are inhibitory (glycine-releasing) interneurons that receive an excitatory collateral from an alpha motor neuron and send an inhibitory axon back to that neuron (or others in the same motor pool), mediating negative feedback","They release glutamate to excite motor neurons further, receiving an inhibitory collateral from the alpha motor neuron and amplifying its own output"],"answer":2,"exp":"Renshaw cells are inhibitory interneurons in the anterior horn that release glycine; they receive an excitatory collateral from an alpha motor neuron's axon and send an inhibitory axon back to that neuron (or others in the motor pool), mediating a negative feedback mechanism. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What do the muscle spindle and Golgi tendon organ each sense?","choices":["Both structures sense identical information about tendon tension exclusively, differing only in their exact anatomical location within the muscle-tendon unit","The muscle spindle senses muscle length and rate of change in length; the Golgi tendon organ senses tendon tension and rate of change in tension","Neither structure provides feedback to the spinal cord, projecting instead directly and exclusively to the cerebellum for subconscious processing","The muscle spindle senses tendon tension and its rate of change, while the Golgi tendon organ senses muscle length and its rate of change"],"answer":1,"exp":"The muscle spindle (located in the muscle belly) senses muscle length and its rate of change, while the Golgi tendon organ (located in the tendon) senses tendon tension and its rate of change — both feed information mainly to the spinal cord, but also to the cerebrum and cerebellum. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What distinguishes intrafusal muscle fibers from extrafusal fibers?","choices":["Extrafusal fibers make up the bulk of the muscle (innervated by alpha motoneurons) and generate contractile force; intrafusal fibers are smaller, innervated by gamma motoneurons, and encapsulated to form the muscle spindle","Intrafusal fibers are considerably longer than extrafusal fibers, running the entire length of the muscle belly from one tendon to the other","Intrafusal fibers make up the bulk of the muscle, are innervated by alpha motoneurons, and generate the majority of contractile force; extrafusal fibers are smaller, gamma-innervated, and form the encapsulated muscle spindle","Both fiber types are functionally identical, generating equal amounts of contractile force and providing equal sensory feedback to the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"Extrafusal fibers make up the bulk of the muscle, are innervated by alpha motoneurons, and provide contractile force; intrafusal fibers are smaller, innervated by gamma motoneurons, and are encapsulated in sheaths to form the muscle spindle, running in parallel with (but much shorter than) the extrafusal fibers. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the functional difference between nuclear bag and nuclear chain intrafusal fibers?","choices":["Neither fiber type is innervated by sensory afferents at all; both receive purely motor innervation from gamma motoneurons and play no sensory role","Nuclear chain fibers (Type Ia afferents) detect dynamic changes only, while nuclear bag fibers (Type II afferents) detect static length changes","Nuclear bag fibers (Type Ia afferents) detect rate of change in length (dynamic); nuclear chain fibers (Type II afferents) detect static changes in length","Both fiber types detect only static length changes, with neither one contributing to the dynamic, rate-sensitive response of the spindle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nuclear bag intrafusal fibers, innervated by Type Ia sensory afferents, detect the rate of change in length (fast, dynamic changes), while nuclear chain intrafusal fibers, innervated by Type II sensory afferents, detect static changes in length. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How does the muscle spindle oppose an increase in muscle length (stretch)?","choices":["The spindle only responds to muscle shortening rather than stretching, since stretch activates Golgi tendon organs along the tendon exclusively","The spindle's intrafusal fibers directly contract via their own myogenic pacemaker activity without requiring any neural signaling from gamma motoneurons","Stretching the spindle has no effect on alpha motoneurons whatsoever, only altering the firing rate of gamma motoneurons in a separate feedback loop","Stretching the spindle stimulates Type Ia and Type II afferents, which stimulate alpha motoneurons to contract and shorten the entire muscle, opposing the original stretch"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stretching a muscle also stretches its spindle, stimulating Type Ia and Type II sensory afferents; Type Ia fibers stimulate alpha-motoneurons in the spinal cord, causing contraction and shortening of the entire muscle, opposing the original stretch. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why are alpha and gamma motor neurons co-activated during voluntary muscle contraction?","choices":["Co-activation serves no functional purpose at all, since the two motor neuron types fire on entirely independent circuits that never actually interact","To ensure the spindle contracts along with the muscle, keeping the stretch response intact rather than going slack","Only gamma motor neurons are active during voluntary movement, with alpha motor neurons contributing solely during involuntary reflexive contractions","Co-activation prevents any muscle contraction from occurring, since simultaneous alpha and gamma firing produces mutually cancelling inhibitory signals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Alpha and gamma motor neurons are co-activated so that when the main (extrafusal) muscle contracts and shortens, the intrafusal fibers of the spindle also contract, keeping the spindle taut and its stretch response intact — otherwise the spindle would go slack during muscle shortening. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the difference between the static and dynamic response of the muscle spindle?","choices":["Static and dynamic responses are essentially identical, with both Type Ia and Type II afferents responding equally regardless of how fast the stretch occurs","Only Type II afferents contribute to the dynamic response, firing in direct proportion to the rate of length change during a rapid muscle stretch","The dynamic response only occurs when the muscle is completely stationary, since any ongoing movement suppresses Type Ia afferent firing entirely","Static response: Type Ia and Type II afferent firing increases in proportion to degree of stretch when stretched slowly; dynamic response: Type Ia afferent firing increases in proportion to rate of length change when stretched rapidly"],"answer":3,"exp":"The static response occurs when the spindle is stretched slowly, with Type Ia and Type II afferent impulses increasing proportional to the degree of stretch; the dynamic response occurs with rapid stretch, with Type Ia afferent impulses increasing proportional to the rate of length change. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What type of reflex arc is the stretch (patellar-type) reflex, and what afferent fiber mediates it?","choices":["A disynaptic reflex arc mediated by Type II afferent fibers","A polysynaptic reflex arc mediated by Type Ib afferent fibers","A monosynaptic reflex arc mediated by Type Ia afferent fibers","A reflex arc requiring no afferent fibers at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"The stretch reflex is a monosynaptic reflex arc: sudden muscle stretch excites the muscle spindle, and Type Ia afferent fibers directly stimulate alpha-motor neurons, causing muscle contraction while synergistic muscles are activated and antagonists inhibited. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In the patellar (knee-jerk) reflex, what happens to the antagonistic hamstring muscle?","choices":["It contracts simultaneously with the quadriceps via shared input","It contracts before the quadriceps via a separate reflex arc","It has no involvement, remaining passive throughout the reflex","It relaxes via an inhibitory interneuron, while the quadriceps contracts"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the patellar reflex, the antagonistic hamstring (flexor) relaxes via an inhibitory interneuron while the Type Ia afferent directly (monosynaptically) stimulates the alpha-motoneuron causing the quadriceps to contract, producing the leg kick. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What type of reflex arc mediates the Golgi tendon organ's autogenic inhibition reflex, and what afferent fiber is involved?","choices":["A disynaptic reflex arc via Type Ib afferent fibers, producing muscle relaxation in response to high tension","A reflex arc using only gamma motor neuron output, adjusting intrafusal fiber tension rather than producing any change in extrafusal muscle force","A monosynaptic reflex arc via Type Ia afferent fibers, producing sudden muscle contraction rather than the protective relaxation actually seen","No reflex arc is involved at all -- it is a purely local mechanical effect confined entirely to the collagen fibers within the tendon itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Golgi tendon organ mediates a disynaptic reflex arc via Type Ib afferent fibers; increased muscle tension activates the reflex, causing sudden muscle relaxation (autogenic inhibition) that protects against muscle tear at very high tensions, while also equalizing force among muscle fibers across the physiological range. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where are the cell bodies of preganglionic and postganglionic autonomic neurons located?","choices":["Preganglionic cell bodies are located in the dorsal root ganglia outside the CNS, with a heavily myelinated axon projecting directly to the visceral effector","Preganglionic cell body in the brainstem or spinal cord, thinly myelinated axon to the ganglion; postganglionic cell body in the autonomic ganglion, unmyelinated axon to the visceral effector","Both preganglionic and postganglionic cell bodies are located together within the brainstem or spinal cord, with only their combined axon terminating in the ganglion","Postganglionic cell bodies are located in the brainstem or spinal cord, with an unmyelinated axon projecting directly to the visceral effector cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"The preganglionic neuron's cell body is located in the brainstem or spinal cord, with a thinly myelinated axon projecting to an autonomic ganglion; the postganglionic neuron's cell body is located within that ganglion, with an unmyelinated axon projecting to the visceral effector cell. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Where are sympathetic ganglia located compared to parasympathetic ganglia, and what does this mean for fiber length?","choices":["Sympathetic ganglia are close to the spinal cord (short preganglionic, long postganglionic fibers); parasympathetic ganglia are in the effector tissue (long preganglionic, short postganglionic fibers)","Parasympathetic ganglia are located close to the spinal cord, giving long postganglionic and short preganglionic fibers, the reverse of sympathetic organization","Sympathetic ganglia are located in the effector tissue itself, giving short postganglionic and long preganglionic fibers similar to parasympathetic organization","Both ganglia types are located immediately adjacent to the spinal cord, producing uniformly short preganglionic and long postganglionic fibers in both divisions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sympathetic ganglia are located close to the spinal cord, giving short preganglionic and long postganglionic fibers, while parasympathetic ganglia are located in the effector tissues themselves, giving long preganglionic and short postganglionic fibers. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve carries parasympathetic fibers to the pupillary sphincter and ciliary muscle?","choices":["CN III (oculomotor)","CN IX (glossopharyngeal)","CN VII (facial)","CN X (vagus)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Parasympathetic fibers originate from cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X plus the sacral spinal cord: CN III carries fibers to the pupillary sphincter/ciliary muscle, CN VII to nasal/lacrimal/submandibular glands, CN IX to the parotid gland, and CN X to visceral organs. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What defines an \"adrenergic\" nerve versus a \"cholinergic\" nerve?","choices":["Adrenergic nerves release acetylcholine; cholinergic nerves release norepinephrine","Adrenergic nerves release norepinephrine; cholinergic nerves release acetylcholine","Adrenergic nerves are only found in the parasympathetic system","Both terms refer to the same type of nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic nerves release norepinephrine at their endings and are called adrenergic nerves; parasympathetic nerves release acetylcholine at their endings and are called cholinergic nerves. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What neurotransmitter do ALL preganglionic autonomic fibers (both sympathetic and parasympathetic) release?","choices":["Norepinephrine","Epinephrine","Dopamine","Acetylcholine"],"answer":3,"exp":"All preganglionic sympathetic and parasympathetic nerve fibers release acetylcholine, regardless of what their corresponding postganglionic fibers release. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What effect does activation of beta-1 adrenergic receptors have on the heart, and what does beta-2 activation cause?","choices":["Beta-1 increases heart rate and contractility; beta-2 causes bronchial dilation, vasodilation in skeletal muscle, calorigenesis, and glycogenolysis","Beta-1 causes bronchial dilation and vasodilation in skeletal muscle; beta-2 increases heart rate and contractility, opposite the true assignment","Both receptors produce identical vasoconstriction and bronchoconstriction, mediated through the same alpha-adrenergic signaling cascade","Neither receptor affects the heart or lungs; both act exclusively on gastrointestinal smooth muscle motility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Beta-1 receptor activation increases heart rate and contractility; beta-2 receptor activation causes bronchial dilation, dilation of blood vessels in skeletal muscle, calorigenesis, and glycogenolysis. Alpha receptors, by contrast, are located on blood vessels and cause vasoconstriction. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Where are nicotinic acetylcholine receptors found, versus muscarinic receptors?","choices":["Muscarinic receptors are ligand-gated ion channels structurally identical to nicotinic receptors, and both types are found exclusively at the skeletal neuromuscular junction","Nicotinic and muscarinic receptors are found in identical locations, both concentrated at autonomic ganglia and on parasympathetic effector organs alike","Muscarinic receptors are only found at the skeletal neuromuscular junction, where they mediate fast excitatory transmission via ligand-gated channels","Nicotinic receptors are found at synapses between pre- and postganglionic neurons and at the neuromuscular junction; muscarinic receptors are found on effector cells stimulated by postganglionic parasympathetic fibers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nicotinic receptors (ligand-gated ion channels) are found in autonomic ganglia (between pre- and postganglionic neurons) and at the skeletal muscle neuromuscular junction; muscarinic receptors (G protein-coupled, 5 subtypes M1-M5) are found on effector cells stimulated by postganglionic parasympathetic fibers. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Where do parasympathetic preganglionic fibers typically synapse, compared to sympathetic preganglionic fibers?","choices":["Both PNS and SNS fibers synapse exclusively within the sympathetic chain, regardless of division, before a long postganglionic fiber reaches the target organ","PNS fibers synapse in the target organ (except CN III, VII, IX); SNS fibers synapse outside the target organ in the sympathetic chain/ganglion (except the adrenal medulla)","SNS fibers always synapse within the target organ itself (except the adrenal medulla), the exact reverse of the typical parasympathetic pattern","PNS fibers never synapse near the target organ, instead synapsing far away in the sympathetic chain alongside sympathetic preganglionic fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers typically synapse within the target organ (with the exception of CN III, VII, and IX), while sympathetic preganglionic fibers synapse outside the target organ in the sympathetic chain or a ganglion (with the exception of the adrenal medulla). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How does norepinephrine's receptor selectivity differ from epinephrine's?","choices":["NE activates almost exclusively beta-2 receptors, producing bronchodilation and skeletal muscle vasodilation as its primary effect","Neither hormone shows meaningful selectivity for alpha receptors; both act almost exclusively through beta-1 and beta-2 pathways","NE mostly activates alpha receptors (with a small effect on beta); Epi activates alpha and beta receptors about equally","NE activates alpha and beta receptors about equally; Epi mostly activates alpha receptors with only a small effect on beta receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Norepinephrine mostly activates alpha receptors (with a small effect on beta receptors), while epinephrine activates alpha and beta receptors about equally. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is albuterol, and what is it used for clinically?","choices":["A selective alpha-1 agonist used clinically to raise blood pressure or to shrink swollen nasal mucous membranes in decongestants","A selective beta-2 agonist, used as a short-acting bronchodilator for COPD, asthma, and emphysema","A non-selective beta agonist used clinically to treat bradycardia, in contrast to selective beta-2 or beta-1 agents used for other indications","A non-selective alpha blocker used clinically to treat hypertension by preventing vasoconstriction at both alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Albuterol is a selective beta-2 agonist used as a short-acting bronchodilator to treat COPD, asthma, and emphysema, in contrast to isoproterenol (non-selective beta agonist, used for bradycardia) and phenylephrine (selective alpha-1 agonist, used to raise BP or shrink nasal blood vessels). — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What percentage of catecholamines released by the adrenal medulla is epinephrine versus norepinephrine, and how does the duration of circulating catecholamine effects compare to direct sympathetic stimulation?","choices":["80% Epi, 20% NE; effects last 5-10x longer (2-4 minutes) than direct sympathetic stimulation","50% Epi, 50% NE; circulating effects last only about as long as direct sympathetic nerve stimulation itself","20% Epi, 80% NE; effects last only half as long (30-60 seconds) as direct sympathetic stimulation","100% Epi, 0% NE; effects last 5-10x longer (2-4 minutes) than direct sympathetic stimulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The adrenal medulla releases about 80% epinephrine and 20% norepinephrine into the blood; circulating catecholamine effects last 5-10 times longer (2-4 minutes) than the effects of direct sympathetic nerve stimulation. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does epinephrine have a greater effect on stimulating the heart, while norepinephrine has a greater effect on constricting blood vessels?","choices":["Norepinephrine stimulates beta receptors more strongly than epinephrine, giving it the greater effect on the heart, while epinephrine stimulates alpha receptors more strongly, giving it the greater effect constricting vessels -- the reverse of the true relationship","Epinephrine acts exclusively on cardiac beta-1 receptors with no effect anywhere else; norepinephrine acts exclusively on vascular alpha receptors with no effect on the heart or metabolism at all","Epinephrine stimulates beta receptors more (heart); norepinephrine stimulates alpha receptors more (vasoconstriction, increasing TPR and arterial pressure)","Both hormones have identical receptor selectivity, stimulating alpha and beta receptors in exactly the same proportion and producing indistinguishable cardiovascular effects"],"answer":2,"exp":"Epinephrine has a greater effect stimulating the heart due to greater beta receptor stimulation, while norepinephrine has a greater effect constricting blood vessels due to greater alpha receptor stimulation (increasing total peripheral resistance and arterial pressure); epinephrine also increases metabolic rate 5-10x more than norepinephrine. — Lecture 5 - Nervous System - Central Nervous System Function-2.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
